title op3: a renamed directory whose child also renames itself

# The severe half of the gap, and the case that was INEXPRESSIBLE in
# full-path coordinates rather than merely awkward.
#
# Off-of renames /d to /e and, inside it, renames f to g.  Both
# dnodes survive, so red carries the directory and red carries the
# child.  Onto is quiet.
#
# In full-path coordinates the child's edit had no writable source.
# "rename /d/f to /e/g" names a source that the directory rename has
# already destroyed.  "rename /e/f to /e/g" names /e/f -- a path that
# appears in NO tree, and the kernel's name table is a rank into the
# union of the three trees, so it cannot mint one.  This is why the
# fix was parked instead of written, on both sides.
#
# Remark 16.5 dissolves it.  The edit is one leaf rename, f to g,
# written inside the moved directory's scope; the applier's position
# supplies the prefix, so no intermediate path is ever named.  The
# parent-first arrow of Definition 12.1 already orders it after the
# directory's own rename -- no new arrow is needed.
#
# Hand-worked: clean.  The gold below is the same under either
# coordinate system.  The difference is only whether the edit list
# can be written down at all, which is exactly the kind of defect a
# gold section cannot catch and a contract has to.

tree base
  1-1: /
    2-100: d/
      3-100: f = fv0

tree onto
  1-1: /
    2-100: d/
      3-100: f = fv0

tree offof
  1-1: /
    2-100: e/
      3-100: g = fv0

# --- expected result, worked by hand 2026-08-31 ---
expect clean

tree expected
  1-1: /
    2-100: e
      3-100: g = fv0
